# The ksystemstats plugin is a compiled shared object, so the package stopped
# being architecture-independent. The apt repo matches on the new _amd64.deb
# asset name.
Architecture: any
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, ${shlibs:Depends},
	python3-pyside6.qtwidgets (>= 6.4),
	fancontrol,
	pkexec,
	ksystemstats,
Recommends: pipewire-bin,
	pulseaudio-utils,
	python3-numpy,
Suggests: nvidia-smi,
	systemsettings,
Description: fan control and system monitor for Juno (Clevo) laptops
 The whole fan stack for this laptop family in one package: the fan-profile
 CLI (quiet/balanced/cool/turbo/auto), the systemd drop-in that re-resolves
 drifting hwmon indices before fancontrol starts, the suspend/resume hook, a
 KDE/Qt6 curve editor and a system-tray monitor.
 .
 The editor shows CPU temperature, fan RPM and PWM duty live, with a draggable
 pwm = f(temp) chart matching the fancontrol control law. Clicking the plot adds
 a control point, turning the two-handle preset into a multi-point curve that is
 driven through an executable FCTEMPS source, since fancontrol itself
 interpolates only one linear segment. Every colour in both GUIs comes from the
 active KDE colour scheme, and the editor is reachable from System Settings as
 well as from the launcher. Presets are scraped
 from the fan-profile script itself so GUI and CLI cannot drift apart. Changes
 go through a root helper (pkexec/polkit) that writes /etc/fancontrol in
 fan-profile's byte format and restarts fancontrol.service, rolling back both
 the file and the daemon if the new config is rejected.
 .
 The tray monitor reports CPU and GPU temperature, fan speed, network
 throughput, discrete-GPU power state, total system power and battery runtime,
 with a rolling CPU/iGPU/dGPU utilization chart.
 .
 Requires the clevofan driver. fan-calibrate (microphone-based PWM noise
 calibration) needs the recommended pipewire-bin, pulseaudio-utils and
 python3-numpy; the tray reads the discrete GPU through the suggested
 nvidia-smi when one is present and awake.
